Title: Diagnosis
Details:
- Failing test: org.apache.commons.math.util.MathUtilsTest.testArrayEquals() asserts that two arrays containing NaN at the same position are not equal:
  assertFalse(MathUtils.equals(new double[] { Double.NaN }, new double[] { Double.NaN }));
- Issue report: “NaN in 'equals' methods” notes that some equals methods return true if both arguments are NaN, which contradicts IEEE-754 (NaN != NaN).
- Code inspection:
  - MathUtils.equals(double[], double[]) iterates and delegates element-wise comparison to equals(double, double):
    for (int i = 0; i < x.length; ++i) {
        if (!equals(x[i], y[i])) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
  - MathUtils.equals(double, double) explicitly returns true when both values are NaN:
    return (Double.isNaN(x) && Double.isNaN(y)) || x == y;
- How the bug occurs:
  - For inputs [NaN] and [NaN], equals(double[], double[]) calls equals(NaN, NaN).
  - equals(double, double) returns true due to the (Double.isNaN(x) && Double.isNaN(y)) branch.
  - Therefore, equals(double[], double[]) returns true, contradicting the test’s expectation and IEEE-754 semantics that NaN != NaN.
- Root cause: Special-casing NaN as equal in MathUtils.equals(double, double) causes array equality to incorrectly treat NaN pairs as equal.

